WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:02.200
Dario, I'm afraid to stick my hand in.

2
00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:02.980
Quick, quick, quick!

3
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:05.420
Quick, quick, quick!

4
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.940
Let's talk about expenses in the village and in the city.

5
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.860
Ten times more money: parking, food, everything.

6
00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:20.010
We're talking about expenses in the village and in the city, basically, it's finance.

7
00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:23.450
At first glance finance seems to me something very complicated.

8
00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:25.170
Like we ordinary people don't deal with it.

9
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.850
It's only people in big buildings, with a million floors.

10
00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:29.610
They deal with finance.

11
00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.270
And we don't have to think about it.

12
00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:34.850
But since finance should be dealt with not only by those people but also by us,

13
00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.070
Daria and I want to check

14
00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:40.030
how we manage organizing our finances in the city

15
00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:44.890
and how my grandmother manages organizing her finances in the village.

16
00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:48.110
In the city you spend on a much larger number of little things,

17
00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:52.290
and when it all adds up in the end, it comes to a not-small sum compared to the village.

18
00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:53.010
But let's see.

19
00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:56.510
Well, the first thing that comes to mind is food, so... lunch.

20
00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:00.280
I have nothing to do with that.

21
00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:02.760
Let's go shopping.

22
00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:03.460
Let's go.

23
00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.440
This is not staged at all, I'm not going back for the camera.

24
00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:08.220
And I never go out in socks at all.

25
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.160
Anyway, I must say: thank you, Erste Bank, for supporting our channel.

26
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.100
I myself lived from paycheck to paycheck.

27
00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.400
I'm ashamed to say it, but it's true.

28
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:20.680
And literally while we were filming the videos, we learned a few things ourselves.

29
00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.800
The site ersteznali.rs, we've mentioned it more than once.

30
00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:28.540
And it's such a good source of information to just solve problems with money.

31
00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:29.800
Most of them.

32
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.780
We lived, basically, from paycheck to paycheck.

33
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.400
And now we're already planning the next trip.

34
00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:41.190
We need some paper towels.

35
00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:42.750
Let's get some big roll.

36
00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:44.130
There's this jumbo one, look.

37
00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:45.610
This is moist toilet paper.

38
00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.090
I like to make sauce from boiled tomatoes.

39
00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.130
64 dinars, and with a discount at that.

40
00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.010
99.99 dinars per kilo.

41
00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:57.170
Bro, potatoes are really...

42
00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:02.410
Look here.

43
00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:04.110
330 dinars.

44
00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:07.010
Sauerkraut, a kilogram — 330 dinars.

45
00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.350
And this one head — 500 dinars.

46
00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:10.150
Oops.

47
00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:11.730
And one head — 500 dinars.

48
00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:12.150
Okay.

49
00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:12.770
Take it?

50
00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:13.650
No, no.

51
00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:14.310
Expensive.

52
00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:18.210
10 eggs — 220 dinars.

53
00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:19.970
Standard, an egg — 22 dinars.

54
00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:22.710
Chicken fillet, 800 dinars per kilogram.

55
00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:25.190
We'll take 500 grams.

56
00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:26.490
That's 400 dinars.

57
00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:27.630
Expensive, expensive.

58
00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:30.370
Why is this an important shot, explain.

59
00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:31.910
So I don't have to carry it.

60
00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:45.820
Well, how is it?

61
00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:46.940
Very tasty.

62
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:48.720
The potatoes were 100 dinars,

63
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.000
but since I used half a kilo, that's 50 dinars.

64
00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:51.520
Good.

65
00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:56.100
Flour — 85, I used it for this dish, but just a little.

66
00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:57.580
Oil — 200 dinars.

67
00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.720
Chicken — 400 dinars.

68
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.620
Sauce, these tomatoes — 64 dinars.

69
00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:02.820
Yes, breadcrumbs.

70
00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:05.080
The breadcrumbs were around 50 dinars.

71
00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.080
Anyway, I calculated everything: total 1070 dinars.

72
00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.240
All the ingredients, all the products.

73
00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:11.440
But you have leftovers, right?

74
00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:14.980
There are leftovers, but in the store I spent 1070 dinars.

75
00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.560
Let's see how much my grandmother spends.

76
00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:20.140
Basically, my grandmother will cook, let's say, a similar lunch.

77
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.840
I just want to eat, I have nothing to say. Really.

78
00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.620
Go on, try it now, and tomorrow you'll tell me which is better — mine or grandma's.

79
00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.560
Since I have to live with you afterwards, not with grandma...

80
00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.640
You cook better than anyone I know.

81
00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:34.280
Really.

82
00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.120
You cook better than my mom.

83
00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:38.620
Kidding.

84
00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.820
This is one thing I don't understand why it exists.

85
00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:43.640
It's the sauce.

86
00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:46.000
It's the tomato sauce that my grandmother makes

87
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.880
and that I made well only once in my life.

88
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:49.580
And never again.

89
00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:52.060
And I think this time too I made it badly.

90
00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.400
Because it smells very strange.

91
00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.380
This sauce is eaten with potatoes.

92
00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:01.760
Maybe it'll do.

93
00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:04.940
All right, we spent 1070 dinars.

94
00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.600
And why are you angry, you're not the one who spent it.

95
00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:17.950
My grandmother has no dogs, so she doesn't spend money on them.

96
00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:20.330
Their food, look, they didn't eat it all.

97
00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:20.910
Look.

98
00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:22.870
Shame.

99
00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:37.520
The stew is ready.

100
00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:41.560
This is your farm.

101
00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:42.440
Should I do this?

102
00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:42.840
Yes, yes.

103
00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:46.100
220 dinars for 10 eggs in the store.

104
00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:49.680
Right?

105
00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:50.280
Yes, right.

106
00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:55.880
Oh, I'm scared.

107
00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:57.220
Dario, I'm afraid to stick my hand in.

108
00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:01.820
Three, one is warm.

109
00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:03.500
Three, three.

110
00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:05.220
So it was just laid.

111
00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:07.020
Seven, eight.

112
00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:08.140
Maybe it's a rat.

113
00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:10.760
Jule!

114
00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:14.980
Jule, don't!

115
00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:16.320
Quick, quick, quick, quick!

116
00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:24.620
Is it worth 220 dinars to suffer like this?

117
00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:32.710
Now we've arrived here, at grandma's yard.

118
00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:35.250
I hope grandma doesn't charge for parking, I'll ask now.

119
00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:38.270
Grandma, is parking in your yard paid?

120
00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:41.210
It's priceless. It's a reserved spot like that.

121
00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:41.850
Of course.

122
00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:43.850
Parking — zero dinars.

123
00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:44.510
Zero, zero.

124
00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:45.630
Private property.

125
00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:48.150
A combine harvester fits here, and a plane can land.

126
00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:08.230
Good.

127
00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:09.970
Lard.

128
00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:11.910
Ribs, look, ribs.

129
00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:17.050
Look here.

130
00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:18.590
Sausage.

131
00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:20.370
Oh, we could take one.

132
00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:23.310
All this — I don't know how much it costs in the store,

133
00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:25.750
but grandma produces everything herself, grandma and grandpa.

134
00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:27.410
So that's life in the village.

135
00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:28.810
You always have meat for food.

136
00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:31.490
You have to crack nuts, I didn't like that.

137
00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:31.630
Nuts.

138
00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:36.010
They grow the potatoes that I paid 100 dinars a kilo for yesterday.

139
00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:38.210
Of all the jobs in the village, which did you not like doing?

140
00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:39.490
Tossing corn.

141
00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:40.190
Here.

142
00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:41.810
There was corn here, it's gone now.

143
00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:43.730
Oh, corn cobs, bro.

144
00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:45.190
How I hated corn cobs.

145
00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:47.550
Stuffing corn cobs into the cellar and into sacks.

146
00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:48.950
The worst.

147
00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:57.120
These are things you can't buy, and that's the point.

148
00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:08.270
Aha.

149
00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:09.410
And this is homemade.

150
00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:10.510
And this one is store-bought.

151
00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:20.310
When I'm a guest at your place, I always pour the soup first.

152
00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:23.790
And tell me, is there something you do here in the village

153
00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:27.330
and think to yourself: I'd rather buy it than waste time and nerves doing this?

154
00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:30.030
No, there isn't. I don't buy anything ready-made.

155
00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:30.950
And pizza?

156
00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:35.550
Pizza is good, yes.

157
00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:37.930
Pizza isn't bad, I don't make it.

158
00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:41.590
And when you were young, did you have such expenses —

159
00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:43.010
you always lived in the village, right?

160
00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:43.230
Yes, yes.

161
00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:46.370
Did you have any expenses, besides what you do on the farm,

162
00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:48.350
did you buy any snacks or something like that?

163
00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:54.950
Roux.

164
00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:57.270
There, like that. There, like that.

165
00:07:57.370 --> 00:07:57.470
Good.

166
00:07:57.910 --> 00:07:58.230
Pour it in.

167
00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:00.090
That's the best recipe for you: everything as needed.

168
00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:00.790
Of course.

169
00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:04.870
And now take that bottle, fill it with water, shake it and pour it in here.

170
00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:06.410
Nothing is thrown away.

171
00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:13.650
Okay, bring sugar.

172
00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:22.310
Good, good, great, great.

173
00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:25.850
The main thing that I, let's say — I don't know how to explain —

174
00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:29.190
it's not that it's missing, but I really notice it in our life in the city:

175
00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:31.510
When we lived in the village, nothing was thrown away.

176
00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:34.470
All leftovers always go either to the dogs or to the pigs.

177
00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:34.990
Yes, yes, yes.

178
00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:36.030
And we throw everything away.

179
00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:40.470
And then you technically save much more, because, you see, all leftovers get eaten.

180
00:08:40.670 --> 00:08:41.050
Yes, yes, yes.

181
00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:43.730
You peel potatoes — we throw it in the trash.

182
00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:45.810
And in the village you peel potatoes — it goes to the pigs.

183
00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:49.830
And we give the dogs dog food, you see, we don't give our leftovers,

184
00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:50.990
we throw our leftovers away.

185
00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:54.070
We cooked roughly the same lunch yesterday:

186
00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:57.310
we cooked chicken, cooked potatoes and cooked sauce.

187
00:08:57.310 --> 00:09:00.270
And guess how much it cost us with the groceries?

188
00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:00.890
I don't know.

189
00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:02.290
1070 dinars.

190
00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:04.630
Yes.

191
00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:10.380
How many kilos do they eat?

192
00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:14.040
One hen eats a hundred and thirty grams. Per day.

193
00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:14.720
Per day, yes.

194
00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:17.360
Because the corn is my own.

195
00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:17.780
Aha.

196
00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:22.160
From that you get 250–300 eggs, so an egg comes out much cheaper.

197
00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:23.220
About 10 dinars per egg.

198
00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:24.920
That's when you produce your own.

199
00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:26.260
Plus the eggs are better.

200
00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:30.740
Yesterday we were at the store, the cabbage was around 500 dinars, right? One head.

201
00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:32.360
That's some kind of golden cabbage.

202
00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:36.520
Now we're going for cabbage so grandma and grandpa can ferment it.

203
00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:50.110
Grandma, who handles the finances?

204
00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:51.450
Who's the boss in the house?

205
00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:57.090
We found five budget myths on the Erste Znali site.

206
00:09:57.370 --> 00:09:59.970
And now grandma will read all these five myths,

207
00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:02.170
and we'll explain whether it's true or not.

208
00:10:02.950 --> 00:10:03.870
Yes. What's the first myth?

209
00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:08.310
So, the first myth: a budget must be super detailed.

210
00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:09.870
It doesn't have to be super detailed.

211
00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:14.170
You don't need to write down exactly: oh, today I had one coffee, and there, I don't know, this and that.

212
00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:14.970
Yes, well, okay.

213
00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:17.450
What matters is that you group things the way that's convenient for you.

214
00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:17.910
True.

215
00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:21.050
And in the end it's important to know what your expenses are, how much you spent.

216
00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:22.170
That's basically the most important thing.

217
00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:22.690
Exactly.

218
00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:34.490
Keeping a budget record takes too much time.

219
00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:37.530
Well, it shouldn't take much time, it's like brushing your teeth.

220
00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:39.710
That is, you do it on autopilot:

221
00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:42.570
aha, got up, brushed teeth, same with this — I keep track every day.

222
00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:43.370
Of course.

223
00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:45.130
What I need to buy, I buy.

224
00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:45.390
Yes.

225
00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:46.150
And that's it.

226
00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:48.550
And that's important, yes, because that way you prevent a disaster,

227
00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:51.630
well, so as not to spend more than you earn.

228
00:10:51.790 --> 00:10:55.530
I don't need to write it down. I know exactly where I spend money.

229
00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:57.890
Well, you know what they say: the wise write it down...

230
00:10:57.990 --> 00:10:58.950
The fool remembers, the wise writes it down!

231
00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:09.650
True.

232
00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:39.850
Why is this important? It's important to write it down,

233
00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:42.830
because then you have everything on paper — how much you spent,

234
00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:45.250
so you know how much your expenses were.

235
00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:47.010
If you memorize, you'll forget.

236
00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:48.870
If you write it down, you know exactly how much.

237
00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:50.030
If I start planning a budget,

238
00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:52.390
I'll never go to the city again.

239
00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:53.950
Yes, but actually that's not so.

240
00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:57.770
If you plan a budget, you'll have money left, and you'll be able to go to the city.

241
00:11:57.950 --> 00:12:00.070
Well, okay, I go to the city, it's not that I don't go.

242
00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:05.150
Unplanned expenses are impossible to avoid.

243
00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:07.850
That's partly true, yes, it's impossible to avoid,

244
00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:09.550
something unplanned can always happen.

245
00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:10.050
Really.

246
00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:15.030
But when for months and years, let's say, you keep your finances and monitor them,

247
00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:18.190
you can always notice some deviations.

248
00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:20.670
Like see where these unplanned expenses go,

249
00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:22.330
so you can control them to some extent.

250
00:12:22.790 --> 00:12:24.770
Yes, they're always there, but you can control them.

251
00:12:24.830 --> 00:12:27.430
Well, and you account for the unplanned, set aside a little.

252
00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:29.410
Yes, right, exactly.

253
00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:31.910
We've gone through five budget myths,

254
00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:36.430
and if you want to learn more about personal finance, about myths and all the other topics,

255
00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:38.010
you can go to the Erste Znali site

256
00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:42.230
and see everything there — not only about personal finance, but other topics too.

257
00:12:42.530 --> 00:12:46.130
Do you have any advice for people about finances and budgeting?

258
00:12:46.250 --> 00:12:48.870
Take everything life gives you.

259
00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:51.950
Praise the village, but live in the city.

260
00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:08.290
And now I have a saying for you, and you tell me whether it's good or not.

261
00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:12.130
Beware of small expenses. A small leak will sink a great ship.

262
00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:14.070
Well, of course.

263
00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:18.590
Little by little.

264
00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:26.070
This quote, "Beware of small expenses, a small leak will sink a great ship,"

265
00:13:26.070 --> 00:13:27.670
was said by Benjamin Franklin.

266
00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:31.450
You can find many such quotes and interesting facts on the Erste Znali site.

267
00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:34.590
You can read much more about what we talked about today in this video,

268
00:13:34.590 --> 00:13:35.990
where we compared village and city.

269
00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:39.410
There, grandpa said you should praise the village but live in the city.

270
00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:42.710
I think in the end expenses are higher in the city, definitely.

271
00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:48.690
Parking, food — all the groceries we bought for lunch, we ate here too,

272
00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:51.670
but here it's homemade production, so less is spent.

273
00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:55.050
As for expenses, in the village, I think, there are definitely fewer.

274
00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:56.830
What do you think, grandma, are there fewer expenses in the village?

275
00:13:56.850 --> 00:14:00.970
Fewer expenses, of course, in the village there are fewer,

276
00:14:00.970 --> 00:14:05.510
but you still have to work, you create it yourself.

277
00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:12.630
You have to plant, first work the land, then plant, then hoe.

278
00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:14.110
Everything has its pros and cons.

279
00:14:21.030 --> 00:14:23.210
Praise the village, but live in the city.

280
00:14:23.390 --> 00:14:24.690
Good, that's good.

281
00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:25.350
True.